#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724144 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 3 x + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\ - y + e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 2$, $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\ 0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -1, 2 and y bounds 0, 2](images/30/306187a2ff03bdf4b7f3c52090b8b5b353902213d297292f200e839df7fea8e4.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=3,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=2.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[2 x\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[6 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\\\\n&=\\left(12\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724145 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - 3 x + e^{y},\\ y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-2\\le x\\le 0$, $-2\\le y\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 0,\\ -2\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -2, 0 and y bounds -2, 1](images/ce/ce4b4868957375d8b936fe85217dda7c29f782af68464900113b0cd374cf2715.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=-3,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=-2.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{-2}^{1}\\int_{-2}^{0}\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-2}^{1}\\left[- 2 x\\right]_{x=-2}^{x=0}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-2}^{1}\\left(-4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[- 4 y\\right]_{y=-2}^{y=1}\\\\\n&=\\left(-4\\right)-\\left(8\\right)\\\\\n&=-12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724146 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - 2 x + e^{y},\\ - 3 y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 2$, $-1\\le y\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\ -1\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -1, 2 and y bounds -1, 1](images/e9/e95eba59b4da9ca3035998f82c261a8a64e09c46ed68178e4789372e45f3deb9.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=-2,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=-5.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(-5\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left[- 5 x\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(-15\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[- 15 y\\right]_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\\\\n&=\\left(-15\\right)-\\left(15\\right)\\\\\n&=-30.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724147 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},\\ 3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1$, $0\\le y\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\ 0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -1, 1 and y bounds 0, 1](images/07/070d1d189359dd70ebdca830d7ee89c2f4cbfeddafa3508f077aaaa3dbe9f059.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=2,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=5.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(5\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[5 x\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=1}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(10\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[10 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\\\\n&=\\left(10\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=10.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724154 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle xz,2y,xz\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,uv,v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. If\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You may use $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle v,-1,u\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "For the stated orientation, the flux integrand is the dot product with the supplied cross product, $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\iint_S \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u$, $y=uv$, and $z=v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle uv,2uv,uv\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N&=(uv)v+(2uv)(-1)+(uv)u\\\\&=uv^2-2uv+u^2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D(uv^2-2uv+u^2v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724165 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle xz,3y,1\\rangle,\\qquad\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle uv,u+v,1-v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. If\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D(auv^2+bv^2+cuv-u+v)\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?" :question/worked-solution "For the stated orientation, use $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$ in\n\n$$\n\\iint_S \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nFirst compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u=\\langle v,1,0\\rangle,\\qquad\\mathbf r_v=\\langle u,1,-1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTheir cross product is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\v&1&0\\\\u&1&-1\\end{vmatrix}=\\langle-1,v,v-u\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=uv$, $y=u+v$, and $z=1-v$ gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle uv(1-v),3(u+v),1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNow form and simplify the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N&=-uv(1-v)+3v(u+v)+(v-u)\\\\&=-uv+uv^2+3uv+3v^2+v-u\\\\&=uv^2+3v^2+2uv-u+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients with the given integrand, $a=1$, $b=3$, and $c=2$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+3+2=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724167 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle z,y,xz\\rangle,\\qquad\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,-u^2v,v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ so that its unit normal has a **positive $y$-component**. If\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. Begin with the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u=\\langle1,-2uv,0\\rangle,\\qquad\\mathbf r_v=\\langle0,-u^2,1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute their ordered cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&-2uv&0\\\\0&-u^2&1\\end{vmatrix}=\\langle-2uv,-1,-u^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nIts $y$-component is negative. Reverse the direction to obtain the oriented area vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_v\\times\\mathbf r_u=-\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle2uv,1,u^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe flux is then expressed using\n\n$$\n\\iint_S \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u$, $y=-u^2v$, and $z=v$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle v,-u^2v,uv\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTake the dot product with the correctly oriented area vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N&=v(2uv)+(-u^2v)(1)+(uv)u^2\\\\&=2uv^2-u^2v+u^3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D(2uv^2-u^2v+u^3v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$ without reversing it would give the negative of this integrand. The unit-normal length factor cancels against the surface area factor, so no extra norm is required."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724173 :question/problem "Evaluate the flux\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\langle x,y,z\\rangle\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nthrough the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,v,1+u+v\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le u\\le2,\\quad0\\le v\\le1.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v.$\n\n*Hint:* You may use $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle-1,-1,1\\rangle$ and the formula\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For the specified orientation, the parametric flux formula is\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u,$ $y=v,$ and $z=1+u+v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle u,v,1+u+v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the supplied vector product, the flux integrand is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)=-u-v+(1+u+v)=1.\n$$\n\nThe two variable terms cancel, so integrate the constant over the parameter rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S&=\\int_0^2\\int_0^1 1\\,\\mathrm dv\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\int_0^2[v]_{0}^{1}\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=[u]_0^2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product already incorporates the surface area scale. Do not normalize it or multiply by its magnitude again."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724174 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\langle x,y,z+1\\rangle\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,v,uv\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le u\\le2,\\quad0\\le v\\le1.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v.$\n\n*Hint: You may use $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle-v,-u,1\\rangle.$*" :question/worked-solution "For the specified orientation, use\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nThe vector product is supplied. First substitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle u,v,uv+1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTake the dot product, keeping both negative components of the normal vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)&=u(-v)+v(-u)+(uv+1)\\\\&=1-uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand varies across the parameter rectangle. Integrate it over the given bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S&=\\int_0^2\\int_0^1(1-uv)\\,\\mathrm dv\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\int_0^2\\left[v-\\frac{uv^2}{2}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\int_0^2\\left(1-\\frac u2\\right)\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\left[u-\\frac{u^2}{4}\\right]_0^2\\\\&=2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724183 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\langle x,y,z+1\\rangle\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u+v,u-v,uv\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n0\\le u\\le1,\\qquad0\\le v\\le2.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v.$" :question/worked-solution "For the specified orientation, the flux is\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nCompute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u=\\langle1,1,v\\rangle,\\qquad\\mathbf r_v=\\langle1,-1,u\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTheir cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&1&v\\\\1&-1&u\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\langle u+v,-(u-v),-1-1\\rangle\\\\&=\\langle u+v,v-u,-2\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u+v,$ $y=u-v,$ and $z=uv$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle u+v,u-v,uv+1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nLet $g(u,v)$ denote the flux integrand. Taking the dot product gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=(u+v)^2+(u-v)(v-u)-2(uv+1)\\\\&=(u+v)^2-(u-v)^2-2uv-2\\\\&=2uv-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate over the parameter rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S&=\\int_0^1\\int_0^2(2uv-2)\\,\\mathrm dv\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\int_0^1[uv^2-2v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=\\int_0^1(4u-4)\\,\\mathrm du\\\\&=[2u^2-4u]_0^1\\\\&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe negative result is signed flux in the specified orientation. Reversing the cross-product order would reverse its sign."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724396 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=2x-1,&y&=2x-4,\\\\x&=2y+2,&x&=2y+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1: Choose a change of variables.** Rearrange each pair of boundaries to expose its shared expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y&=1,&2x-y&=4,\\\\x-2y&=2,&x-2y&=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $u=2x-y$ and $v=x-2y$.\n\n**Step 2: Map the boundaries.** They become $u=1$, $u=4$, $v=2$, and $v=6$, giving\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\leq u\\leq4,\\ 2\\leq v\\leq6\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangular region.](images/bd/bde331a044869e44b1a53ffea146b9c99253678024dda3ce1987700fb69827d6.png)\n\n**Step 3: Find the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}=\\begin{vmatrix}2&-1\\\\1&-2\\end{vmatrix}=-4+1=-3.\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Find the area scale factor.** The determinant is nonzero. The inverse is $x=(2u-v)/3$, $y=(u-2v)/3$, so the transformation is one-to-one. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=-\\frac13,\\qquad \\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac13.\n$$\n\n**Step 5: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\frac13\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\int_2^6\\int_1^4\\frac13\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\frac13(4-1)(6-2)=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724402 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y&=1,&x+y&=3,\\\\x-y&=0,&x-y&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1: Choose a change of variables.** The same expression occurs on each pair of parallel boundaries. Choose\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\qquad v=x-y.\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Map the boundaries.** The four lines become $u=1$, $u=3$, $v=0$, and $v=2$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\leq u\\leq3,\\ 0\\leq v\\leq2\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangular region.](images/2b/2bf7a1e04cdcecfb7bfe3074191421fb24fe35bb973d762fab6b18e9049221b2.png)\n\n**Step 3: Find the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}=\\begin{vmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{vmatrix}=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Find the area scale factor.** The inverse is $x=(u+v)/2$, $y=(u-v)/2$, so this is an invertible linear change of variables. Its inverse Jacobian is $-1/2$; area uses its absolute value, $1/2$.\n\n**Step 5: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\int_0^2\\int_1^3\\frac12\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\frac12(3-1)(2-0)=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724406 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac3x,\\qquad y=\\frac7x,\\qquad x=y^2,\\qquad x=8y^2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1: Choose a change of variables.** Since $x,y>0$, the boundary equations can be written as\n\n$$\nxy=3,\\qquad xy=7,\\qquad \\frac{x}{y^2}=1,\\qquad \\frac{x}{y^2}=8.\n$$\n\nChoose $u=xy$ and $v=x/y^2$.\n\n**Step 2: Map the boundaries.** The transformed region is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\leq u\\leq7,\\ 1\\leq v\\leq8\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangular region.](images/4f/4f46dbee2037dfa93fdd4e020a25e3d1386ec78332f4e713ee69c51e32128b36.png)\n\nFor $u,v>0$, the unique positive inverse is $y=(u/v)^{1/3}$ and $x=u^{2/3}v^{1/3}$. It maps this whole rectangle onto the required first-quadrant region.\n\n**Step 3: Find the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}y&x\\\\1/y^2&-2x/y^3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2x}{y^2}-\\frac{x}{y^2}=-3v.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Find the area scale factor.** Since $1\\leq v\\leq8$, the Jacobian never vanishes. Taking the reciprocal and then its absolute value gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=-\\frac{1}{3v},\\qquad\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5: Integrate over the rectangle.** The scale factor varies with $v$; it cannot be pulled outside both integrals as a constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_1^8\\int_3^7\\frac{1}{3v}\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\frac{7-3}{3}\\int_1^8\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm dv\\\\\n&=\\frac43(\\ln8-\\ln1)=4\\ln2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724418 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^2y,\\qquad v=xy^2,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=\\frac1{x^2}, & y&=\\frac8{x^2},\\\\x&=\\frac1{y^2}, & x&=\\frac{27}{y^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map takes $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. Its inverse $T$ takes the rectangle $\\Delta$ back to $D$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\nThe two pairs of curves map to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1/x^2&\\Longrightarrow u=1,\\\\y=8/x^2&\\Longrightarrow u=8,\\\\x=1/y^2&\\Longrightarrow v=1,\\\\x=27/y^2&\\Longrightarrow v=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\leq u\\leq8,\\quad1\\leq v\\leq27\\}.\n$$\n\n\n\n![The transformed rectangle](images/d6/d63b7db25c9091d3667e048dc8e149381b49a805c8b32aa73c0f183f94cb050b.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}2xy&x^2\\\\y^2&2xy\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=4x^2y^2-x^2y^2=3x^2y^2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Find the inverse area factor.**\n\nThe area factor must be written entirely in $u,v$. Multiplying the two supplied expressions gives\n\n$$\nuv=x^3y^3=(xy)^3,\\qquad x^2y^2=(uv)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nSince $x,y>0$, there is no sign ambiguity. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3(uv)^{2/3}}=\\frac13u^{-2/3}v^{-2/3}.\n$$\n\nFor completeness, $x^3=u^2/v$ and $y^3=v^2/u$ give unique positive $x,y$ throughout $\\Delta$. The Jacobian is nonzero there.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\frac13\\int_1^8\\int_1^{27}u^{-2/3}v^{-2/3}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u\\\\&=\\frac13\\left[3u^{1/3}\\right]_1^8\\left[3v^{1/3}\\right]_1^{27}\\\\&=\\frac13\\cdot3(2-1)\\cdot3(3-1)\\\\&=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724424 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x^2}{y},\\qquad v=xy,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=\\frac{x^2}{2}, & y&=\\frac{x^2}{5},\\\\y&=\\frac1x, & y&=\\frac4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map takes $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. Its inverse $T$ takes the rectangle $\\Delta$ back to $D$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\nEach pair becomes a pair of constant-coordinate lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=x^2/2&\\Longrightarrow u=2,\\\\y=x^2/5&\\Longrightarrow u=5,\\\\y=1/x&\\Longrightarrow v=1,\\\\y=4/x&\\Longrightarrow v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\leq u\\leq5,\\quad1\\leq v\\leq4\\}.\n$$\n\n\n\n![The transformed rectangle](images/ce/ce4fe8d57aa88a91d88c848f060885f8840391beb4c34012236843a6bf832e65.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}2x/y&-x^2/y^2\\\\y&x\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\frac{2x^2}{y}+\\frac{x^2}{y}=\\frac{3x^2}{y}=3u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Find the inverse area factor.**\n\nTake the reciprocal and express the area factor in the new coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\frac{1}{3u},\\qquad \\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nHere $u>0$. Also $x^3=uv$ and $y=v/x$ determine a unique positive pair $(x,y)$ for every point of the rectangle, so the change of variables is one-to-one and nonsingular.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_2^5\\int_1^4\\frac{1}{3u}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u\\\\&=\\frac{4-1}{3}\\int_2^5\\frac1u\\,\\mathrm d u\\\\&=\\ln5-\\ln2=\\ln\\left(\\frac52\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724426 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-2x,\\qquad v=y+x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the lines\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=2x-1, & y&=2x+1,\\\\y&=2-x, & y&=4-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map takes $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. Its inverse $T$ takes the rectangle $\\Delta$ back to $D$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\nSubstitute the given expressions into each pair of boundary equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x-1&\\Longrightarrow u=-1,\\\\y=2x+1&\\Longrightarrow u=1,\\\\y=2-x&\\Longrightarrow v=2,\\\\y=4-x&\\Longrightarrow v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):-1\\leq u\\leq1,\\quad 2\\leq v\\leq4\\}.\n$$\n\n\n\n![The transformed rectangle](images/fd/fd928a6596ec40ce28899e4c31f8e37b58e642552e4e239e7db28a4986c57d5c.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}=\\begin{vmatrix}-2&1\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix}=-3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Find the inverse area factor.**\n\nThe determinant of the inverse is the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=-\\frac13,\\qquad \\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac13.\n$$\n\nThe affine map is one-to-one. In fact, $x=(v-u)/3$ and $y=(u+2v)/3$ are positive throughout this rectangle. The determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{2}^{4}\\frac13\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u\\\\&=\\frac13(1-(-1))(4-2)\\\\&=\\frac43.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724576 :question/problem "Evaluate the signed flux\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nfor the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle - y - z,-2,- x - y\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle 2,v^{2},u - v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse the parameter rectangle\n\n$$\n-1\\le u\\le 0,\\qquad 1\\le v\\le 3.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. Enter an exact value.\n\n*Hint:* The oriented area vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle - 2 v,0,0\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\Phi=$ {{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the parametric flux formula with the specified orientation:\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nUse the supplied oriented area vector $\\mathbf N=\\langle - 2 v,0,0\\rangle$.\n\nSubstitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\nx=2,\\quad y=v^{2},\\quad z=u - v.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle - u - v^{2} + v,-2,- v^{2} - 2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nForm the three signed dot-product contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1}&=(- u - v^{2} + v)(- 2 v) \\\\ &=2 u v+2 v^{3} \\\\ &\\quad {}- 2 v^{2} \\\\ T_{2}&=(-2)(0) \\\\ &=0 \\\\ T_{3}&=(- v^{2} - 2)(0) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the polynomial terms, writing $g(u,v)=T_1+T_2+T_3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=2 u v+2 v^{3} \\\\ &\\quad {}- 2 v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive with respect to $v$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(u,v)&=u v^{2}+\\frac{v^{4}}{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{2 v^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both inner endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\int_{1}^{3}g(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\left[P(u,v)\\right]_{v=1}^{v=3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=8 u+\\frac{68}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive of $H(u)$ with respect to $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(u)&=4 u^{2}+\\frac{68 u}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both outer endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{-1}^{0}H(u)\\,\\mathrm du \\\\ &=\\left[Q(u)\\right]_{u=-1}^{u=0} \\\\ &=\\frac{56}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product includes the surface area scale; there is no extra normalization factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724579 :question/problem "Evaluate the signed flux\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nfor the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle - x^{2},-4,6 z - 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,- v,3 u v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse the parameter rectangle\n\n$$\n1\\le u\\le 3,\\qquad 0\\le v\\le 2.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. Enter an exact value.\n\n*Hint:* The oriented area vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle 3 v,- 3 u,-1\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\Phi=$ {{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the parametric flux formula with the specified orientation:\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nUse the supplied oriented area vector $\\mathbf N=\\langle 3 v,- 3 u,-1\\rangle$.\n\nSubstitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\nx=u,\\quad y=- v,\\quad z=3 u v.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle - u^{2},-4,18 u v - 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nForm the three signed dot-product contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1}&=(- u^{2})(3 v) \\\\ &=- 3 u^{2} v \\\\ T_{2}&=(-4)(- 3 u) \\\\ &=12 u \\\\ T_{3}&=(18 u v - 1)(-1) \\\\ &=- 18 u v+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the polynomial terms, writing $g(u,v)=T_1+T_2+T_3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=- 3 u^{2} v- 18 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}+12 u+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive with respect to $v$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(u,v)&=- \\frac{3 u^{2} v^{2}}{2}- 9 u v^{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}+12 u v+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both inner endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\int_{0}^{2}g(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\left[P(u,v)\\right]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=- 6 u^{2}- 12 u \\\\ &\\quad {}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive of $H(u)$ with respect to $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(u)&=- 2 u^{3}- 6 u^{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}+2 u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both outer endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{1}^{3}H(u)\\,\\mathrm du \\\\ &=\\left[Q(u)\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\ &=-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product includes the surface area scale; there is no extra normalization factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724580 :question/problem "Evaluate the signed flux\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nfor the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle - 2 x,- 2 y,2 z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle - u + v,u v,u + v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse the parameter rectangle\n\n$$\n1\\le u\\le 3,\\qquad 1\\le v\\le 2.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$. Enter an exact value.\n\n*Hint:* The oriented area vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle - u + v,2,- u - v\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\Phi=$ {{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the parametric flux formula with the specified orientation:\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nUse the supplied oriented area vector $\\mathbf N=\\langle - u + v,2,- u - v\\rangle$.\n\nSubstitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\nx=- u + v,\\quad y=u v,\\quad z=u + v.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle 2 u - 2 v,- 2 u v,2 u + 2 v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nForm the three signed dot-product contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1}&=(2 u - 2 v)(- u + v) \\\\ &=- 2 u^{2}+4 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}- 2 v^{2} \\\\ T_{2}&=(- 2 u v)(2) \\\\ &=- 4 u v \\\\ T_{3}&=(2 u + 2 v)(- u - v) \\\\ &=- 2 u^{2}- 4 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}- 2 v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the polynomial terms, writing $g(u,v)=T_1+T_2+T_3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=- 4 u^{2}- 4 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}- 4 v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive with respect to $v$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(u,v)&=- 4 u^{2} v- 2 u v^{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{4 v^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both inner endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\int_{1}^{2}g(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\left[P(u,v)\\right]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=- 4 u^{2}- 6 u \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive of $H(u)$ with respect to $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(u)&=- \\frac{4 u^{3}}{3}- 3 u^{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{28 u}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both outer endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{1}^{3}H(u)\\,\\mathrm du \\\\ &=\\left[Q(u)\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\ &=- \\frac{232}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product includes the surface area scale; there is no extra normalization factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724584 :question/problem "Evaluate the signed flux\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nfor the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle x,- y,z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle v,u v,u\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse the parameter rectangle\n\n$$\n1\\le u\\le 2,\\qquad 0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_v\\times\\mathbf r_u$. Enter an exact value.\n\n*Hint:* The oriented area vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_v\\times\\mathbf r_u=\\langle u,-1,v\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\Phi=$ {{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the parametric flux formula with the specified orientation:\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nUse the supplied oriented area vector $\\mathbf N=\\langle u,-1,v\\rangle$.\n\nSubstitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\nx=v,\\quad y=u v,\\quad z=u.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle v,- u v,u\\rangle.\n$$\n\nForm the three signed dot-product contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1}&=(v)(u) \\\\ &=u v \\\\ T_{2}&=(- u v)(-1) \\\\ &=u v \\\\ T_{3}&=(u)(v) \\\\ &=u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the polynomial terms, writing $g(u,v)=T_1+T_2+T_3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=3 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive with respect to $v$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(u,v)&=\\frac{3 u v^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both inner endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\int_{0}^{1}g(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\left[P(u,v)\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\frac{3 u}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive of $H(u)$ with respect to $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(u)&=\\frac{3 u^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both outer endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{1}^{2}H(u)\\,\\mathrm du \\\\ &=\\left[Q(u)\\right]_{u=1}^{u=2} \\\\ &=\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product includes the surface area scale; there is no extra normalization factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724585 :question/problem "Evaluate the signed flux\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S\n$$\n\nfor the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\langle - 2 x,- 2 y,- z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe parametric surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u v,u + v,- u + v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUse the parameter rectangle\n\n$$\n1\\le u\\le 3,\\qquad 0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\nUse the orientation induced by $\\mathbf r_v\\times\\mathbf r_u$. Enter an exact value.\n\n*Hint:* The oriented area vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_v\\times\\mathbf r_u=\\langle -2,u + v,u - v\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$\\Phi=$ {{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "Use the parametric flux formula with the specified orientation:\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv.\n$$\n\nUse the supplied oriented area vector $\\mathbf N=\\langle -2,u + v,u - v\\rangle$.\n\nSubstitute the surface coordinates into the field:\n\n$$\nx=u v,\\quad y=u + v,\\quad z=- u + v.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\langle - 2 u v,- 2 u - 2 v,u - v\\rangle.\n$$\n\nForm the three signed dot-product contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1}&=(- 2 u v)(-2) \\\\ &=4 u v \\\\ T_{2}&=(- 2 u - 2 v)(u + v) \\\\ &=- 2 u^{2}- 4 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}- 2 v^{2} \\\\ T_{3}&=(u - v)(u - v) \\\\ &=u^{2}- 2 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}+v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the polynomial terms, writing $g(u,v)=T_1+T_2+T_3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(u,v)&=- u^{2}- 2 u v \\\\ &\\quad {}- v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive with respect to $v$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(u,v)&=- u^{2} v- u v^{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both inner endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=\\int_{0}^{1}g(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\left[P(u,v)\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(u)&=- u^{2}- u \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA primitive of $H(u)$ with respect to $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(u)&=- \\frac{u^{3}}{3}- \\frac{u^{2}}{2} \\\\ &\\quad {}- \\frac{u}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate both outer endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{1}^{3}H(u)\\,\\mathrm du \\\\ &=\\left[Q(u)\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\ &=- \\frac{40}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product includes the surface area scale; there is no extra normalization factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724604 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle - x^{2},\\,- 2 x y,\\,- 2 z\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u,\\,v,\\,2 u v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D (a u v + b u^{2} v)\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b$?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. First compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u & =\\left\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,2 v\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v & =\\left\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,2 u\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir ordered cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&0&2 v\\\\0&1&2 u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - 2 v,\\,- 2 u,\\,1\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nFor this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u$, $y=v$, $z=2 u v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\left\\langle - u^{2},\\,- 2 u v,\\,- 4 u v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute the three component contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{x} & =\\left(- u^{2}\\right)\\left(- 2 v\\right) \\\\  & =2 u^{2} v \\\\ T_{y} & =\\left(- 2 u v\\right)\\left(- 2 u\\right) \\\\  & =4 u^{2} v \\\\ T_{z} & =\\left(- 4 u v\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\  & =- 4 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N & =T_x+T_y+T_z \\\\  & =6 u^{2} v - 4 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\left(6 u^{2} v - 4 u v\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients gives $a=-4$, $b=6$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b=(-4)+(6)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724612 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle - x z,\\,3 y,\\,-1\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u v,\\,u + v,\\,1 - v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D (a u v^{2} + b v^{2} + c u v + u - v)\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. First compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u & =\\left\\langle v,\\,1,\\,0\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v & =\\left\\langle u,\\,1,\\,-1\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir ordered cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\v&1&0\\\\u&1&-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -1,\\,v,\\,- u + v\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nFor this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u v$, $y=u + v$, $z=1 - v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\left\\langle u v^{2} - u v,\\,3 u + 3 v,\\,-1\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute the three component contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{x} & =\\left(u v^{2} - u v\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\  & =- u v^{2} + u v \\\\ T_{y} & =\\left(3 u + 3 v\\right)\\left(v\\right) \\\\  & =3 u v + 3 v^{2} \\\\ T_{z} & =\\left(-1\\right)\\left(- u + v\\right) \\\\  & =u - v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N & =T_x+T_y+T_z \\\\  & =- u v^{2} + 4 u v + u + 3 v^{2} - v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\left(- u v^{2} + 4 u v + u + 3 v^{2} - v\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients gives $a=-1$, $b=3$, $c=4$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-1)+(3)+(4)=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724613 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle - 3 z,\\,- x z,\\,- 2 y z\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u,\\,u^{2},\\,v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D (a u v + b u^{2} v)\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b$?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. First compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u & =\\left\\langle 1,\\,2 u,\\,0\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v & =\\left\\langle 0,\\,0,\\,1\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir ordered cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&2 u&0\\\\0&0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2 u,\\,-1,\\,0\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nFor this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u$, $y=u^{2}$, $z=v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\left\\langle - 3 v,\\,- u v,\\,- 2 u^{2} v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute the three component contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{x} & =\\left(- 3 v\\right)\\left(2 u\\right) \\\\  & =- 6 u v \\\\ T_{y} & =\\left(- u v\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\  & =u v \\\\ T_{z} & =\\left(- 2 u^{2} v\\right)\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N & =T_x+T_y+T_z \\\\  & =- 5 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\left(- 5 u v\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients gives $a=-5$, $b=0$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b=(-5)+(0)=-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724616 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle -3,\\,0,\\,-3\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u + v,\\,u^{2} + v,\\,u^{3} + 3 v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D (a u^{2} + b u + c)\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. First compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u & =\\left\\langle 1,\\,2 u,\\,3 u^{2}\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v & =\\left\\langle 1,\\,1,\\,3\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir ordered cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&2 u&3 u^{2}\\\\1&1&3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - 3 u^{2} + 6 u,\\,3 u^{2} - 3,\\,1 - 2 u\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nFor this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u + v$, $y=u^{2} + v$, $z=u^{3} + 3 v$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\left\\langle -3,\\,0,\\,-3\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute the three component contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{x} & =\\left(-3\\right)\\left(- 3 u^{2} + 6 u\\right) \\\\  & =9 u^{2} - 18 u \\\\ T_{y} & =\\left(0\\right)\\left(3 u^{2} - 3\\right) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ T_{z} & =\\left(-3\\right)\\left(1 - 2 u\\right) \\\\  & =6 u - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N & =T_x+T_y+T_z \\\\  & =9 u^{2} - 12 u - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\left(9 u^{2} - 12 u - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients gives $a=9$, $b=-12$, $c=-3$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b+c=(9)+(-12)+(-3)=-6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724623 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(u,v)$ for $D=(0,1)\\times(0,1)$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle x + \\frac{2}{z},\\,y + \\frac{3}{z},\\,z\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u e^{v},\\,u e^{- v},\\,u\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nOrient $S$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D (a e^{v} + b e^{- v})\\,\\mathrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b$?" :question/worked-solution "The vector area element must agree with the specified orientation. First compute the tangent vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u & =\\left\\langle e^{v},\\,e^{- v},\\,1\\right\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v & =\\left\\langle u e^{v},\\,- u e^{- v},\\,0\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir ordered cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\e^{v}&e^{- v}&1\\\\u e^{v}&- u e^{- v}&0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle u e^{- v},\\,u e^{v},\\,- 2 u\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$.\n\nFor this orientation,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))\\cdot\\mathbf N(u,v)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=u e^{v}$, $y=u e^{- v}$, $z=u$ into the field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(u,v))=\\left\\langle u e^{v} + \\frac{2}{u},\\,u e^{- v} + \\frac{3}{u},\\,u\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCompute the three component contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{x} & =\\left(u e^{v} + \\frac{2}{u}\\right)\\left(u e^{- v}\\right) \\\\  & =u^{2} + 2 e^{- v} \\\\ T_{y} & =\\left(u e^{- v} + \\frac{3}{u}\\right)\\left(u e^{v}\\right) \\\\  & =u^{2} + 3 e^{v} \\\\ T_{z} & =\\left(u\\right)\\left(- 2 u\\right) \\\\  & =- 2 u^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf N & =T_x+T_y+T_z \\\\  & =3 e^{v} + 2 e^{- v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf S=\\iint_D\\left(3 e^{v} + 2 e^{- v}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nMatching coefficients gives $a=3$, $b=2$. Hence\n\n$$\na+b=(3)+(2)=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724776 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t^3-2t,\\qquad y=2t^2+t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First find the point by substituting $t=-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(-1)&=(-1)^3-2(-1)=1,\\\\\ny(-1)&=2(-1)^2+(-1)=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinate functions:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3t^2-2,\\qquad y'(t)=4t+1.\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{4t+1}{3t^2-2}$. At $t=-1$,\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{4(-1)+1}{3(-1)^2-2}=\\frac{-3}{1}=-3.\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(1,1)$ and slope $-3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-1&=-3(x-1),\\\\\ny&=-3x+4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEnter $-3x+4$, the expression after $y=$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724783 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2t+1,\\qquad y=t^2-3,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe point $P=(5,1)$ corresponds to $t=2$. Calculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at $P$.\n\nUse $m=\\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}$ and the point-slope form $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the two coordinate functions:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2,\\qquad y'(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nAt $t=2$, the slope is\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(2)}{x'(2)}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nThe supplied point agrees with the equations: $x(2)=5$ and $y(2)=1$. Using $(x_1,y_1)=(5,1)$ gives\n\n$$\ny-1=2(x-5).\n$$\n\nThe dashed tangent touches the curve at $P$ and has slope $2$.\n\n![Parametric curve and its tangent at P](images/70/705f566aef076dc57f19e0e92302b08daf4efc1b06124abeb244bb09257a8647.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724784 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t(\\ln t+1),\\qquad y=\\ln t,\\qquad t>0.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point corresponding to $t=e$. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Give exact answers.\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}}$,\\,${{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=e$ to find $C$:\n\n$$\nx(e)=e(\\ln e+1)=2e,\\qquad y(e)=\\ln e=1.\n$$\n\nUse the product rule to differentiate $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=(\\ln t+1)+t\\cdot\\frac{1}{t}=\\ln t+2,\\\\\ny'(t)&=\\frac{1}{t}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope at $C$ is\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(e)}{x'(e)}=\\frac{1/e}{3}=\\frac{1}{3e}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent through $(2e,1)$ therefore has equation\n\n$$\ny-1=\\frac{1}{3e}(x-2e).\n$$\n\nOn the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute this into the tangent equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-1&=\\frac{1}{3e}(0-2e)=-\\frac{2}{3},\\\\\ny&=\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the required intersection is\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\frac{1}{3}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724791 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\qquad v=x y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{6}{x},\\qquad y=5 x^{2}, \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{x},\\qquad y=2 x^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map sends $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. The area formula uses the absolute determinant of its *inverse*.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_\\Delta\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2 x^{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=2. \\\\ y=5 x^{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=5. \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x}&\\Longrightarrow v=1. \\\\ y=\\frac{6}{x}&\\Longrightarrow v=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\leq u\\leq 5,\\quad 1\\leq v\\leq 6\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle; the two axes may use different scales.](images/e8/e8f27760c6528ebae373087463b01217d46d0ae81bdc43cf3899a0d587f89606.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2 y}{x^{3}} & \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=- \\frac{3 y}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Express the inverse area factor in the new coordinates.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{x^{2}}{3 y} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{3 u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3 u}.\n$$\n\nThe positive inverse is unique:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{v}}{\\sqrt[3]{u}},\\qquad y=\\sqrt[3]{u} v^{\\frac{2}{3}}.\n$$\n\nThese expressions are defined throughout the positive rectangle, and the determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\nFor the logarithmic endpoint difference, use $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ and $\\ln(a^m)=m\\ln a$ for positive arguments.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln 5-\\ln 2&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=- \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{2}^{5}\\int_{1}^{6}\\frac{1}{3 u}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left[\\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{2}^{5}\\left[v\\right]_{1}^{6} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}-(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)})\\right)\\left(6-(1)\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724794 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y^{2}}{x},\\qquad v=x y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{6}{x},\\qquad x=\\frac{y^{2}}{5}, \\\\ x &=\\frac{y^{2}}{3},\\qquad y=\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map sends $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. The area formula uses the absolute determinant of its *inverse*.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_\\Delta\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{y^{2}}{3}&\\Longrightarrow u=3. \\\\ x=\\frac{y^{2}}{5}&\\Longrightarrow u=5. \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x}&\\Longrightarrow v=2. \\\\ y=\\frac{6}{x}&\\Longrightarrow v=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\leq u\\leq 5,\\quad 2\\leq v\\leq 6\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle; the two axes may use different scales.](images/e4/e4bff7ea00777fc6f37c45f20c6bef2428f41b1d3d2a5503a17e398c942dfba1.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} & \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=- \\frac{3 y^{2}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Express the inverse area factor in the new coordinates.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{x}{3 y^{2}} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{3 u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3 u}.\n$$\n\nThe positive inverse is unique:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v^{\\frac{2}{3}}}{\\sqrt[3]{u}},\\qquad y=\\sqrt[3]{u} \\sqrt[3]{v}.\n$$\n\nThese expressions are defined throughout the positive rectangle, and the determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\nFor the logarithmic endpoint difference, use $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ and $\\ln(a^m)=m\\ln a$ for positive arguments.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln 5-\\ln 3&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)=- \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{3}^{5}\\int_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{3 u}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left[\\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{3}^{5}\\left[v\\right]_{2}^{6} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}-(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)})\\right)\\left(6-(2)\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724804 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} y,\\qquad v=\\frac{1}{x y},\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{1}{x^{2}},\\qquad y=\\frac{5}{x^{2}}, \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{5 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map sends $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. The area formula uses the absolute determinant of its *inverse*.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_\\Delta\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x^{2}}&\\Longrightarrow u=1. \\\\ y=\\frac{5}{x^{2}}&\\Longrightarrow u=5. \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x}&\\Longrightarrow v=1. \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{5 x}&\\Longrightarrow v=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\leq u\\leq 5,\\quad 1\\leq v\\leq 5\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle; the two axes may use different scales.](images/8b/8b0859278a3e240d0782cb88b878cf75175f2c890f57e520599ea9842c26f8d8.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 x y & x^{2} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{1}{x y^{2}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Express the inverse area factor in the new coordinates.**\n\nThe reciprocal simplifies using the supplied products:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(xy)^2}{x^2y}=\\frac{1}{v^2u}.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- y \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe positive inverse is unique:\n\n$$\nx=u v,\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThese expressions are defined throughout the positive rectangle, and the determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\nFor the logarithmic endpoint difference, use $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ and $\\ln(a^m)=m\\ln a$ for positive arguments.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln 5-\\ln 1&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{1}\\right)=\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{1}^{5}\\int_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\ &=1\\left[\\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{1}^{5}\\left[- \\frac{1}{v}\\right]_{1}^{5} \\\\ &=1\\left(\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}-(0)\\right)\\left(- \\frac{1}{5}-(-1)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724815 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} y,\\qquad v=\\frac{1}{x y},\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{5}{x^{2}},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{3 x}, \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{5 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{2}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map sends $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. The area formula uses the absolute determinant of its *inverse*.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_\\Delta\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{2}{x^{2}}&\\Longrightarrow u=2. \\\\ y=\\frac{5}{x^{2}}&\\Longrightarrow u=5. \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{3 x}&\\Longrightarrow v=3. \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{5 x}&\\Longrightarrow v=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\leq u\\leq 5,\\quad 3\\leq v\\leq 5\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle; the two axes may use different scales.](images/87/872071d31b3820b265dbbccdd68d7ec9c171a01733707a67f9a4b735965657f8.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 x y & x^{2} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{1}{x y^{2}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Express the inverse area factor in the new coordinates.**\n\nThe reciprocal simplifies using the supplied products:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(xy)^2}{x^2y}=\\frac{1}{v^2u}.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- y \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe positive inverse is unique:\n\n$$\nx=u v,\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThese expressions are defined throughout the positive rectangle, and the determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\nFor the logarithmic endpoint difference, use $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ and $\\ln(a^m)=m\\ln a$ for positive arguments.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln 5-\\ln 2&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=- \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{2}^{5}\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{u v^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\ &=1\\left[\\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{2}^{5}\\left[- \\frac{1}{v}\\right]_{3}^{5} \\\\ &=1\\left(\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}-(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)})\\right)\\left(- \\frac{1}{5}-\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{15} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724816 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\qquad v=\\frac{y^{2}}{x},\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=5 x^{2},\\qquad x=\\frac{y^{2}}{3}, \\\\ x &=\\frac{y^{2}}{5},\\qquad y=3 x^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The supplied map sends $(x,y)$ to $(u,v)$. The area formula uses the absolute determinant of its *inverse*.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_\\Delta\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm d u\\,\\mathrm d v.\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Map the boundaries.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3 x^{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=3. \\\\ y=5 x^{2}&\\Longrightarrow u=5. \\\\ x=\\frac{y^{2}}{3}&\\Longrightarrow v=3. \\\\ x=\\frac{y^{2}}{5}&\\Longrightarrow v=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\leq u\\leq 5,\\quad 3\\leq v\\leq 5\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle; the two axes may use different scales.](images/4a/4a6fa2bb18e4ea82f8cd2189490c1d59e2b3437259ffd1107bf96c79d5a05f2e.png)\n\n**Step 2: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2 y}{x^{3}} & \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\\\ - \\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} & \\frac{2 y}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=- \\frac{3 y^{2}}{x^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Express the inverse area factor in the new coordinates.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{x^{4}}{3 y^{2}} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{3 u^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3 u^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe positive inverse is unique:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{v}}{u^{\\frac{2}{3}}},\\qquad y=\\frac{v^{\\frac{2}{3}}}{\\sqrt[3]{u}}.\n$$\n\nThese expressions are defined throughout the positive rectangle, and the determinant never vanishes.\n\n**Step 4: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_{3}^{5}\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{3 u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left[- \\frac{1}{u}\\right]_{3}^{5}\\left[v\\right]_{3}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(- \\frac{1}{5}-\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)\\left(5-(3)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{4}{45}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724831 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=3 x + 1,\\qquad y=3 x - 1, \\\\ x&=y - 1,\\qquad x=y + 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1: Choose coordinates.** Rewrite each boundary using its shared expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 x - y&=-1,\\qquad 3 x - y=1, \\\\ x - y&=-1,\\qquad x - y=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $u=3 x - y$ and $v=x - y$.\n\n**Step 2: Map the boundaries.** The four boundaries become $u=-1$, $u=1$, $v=-1$, and $v=4$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):-1\\leq u\\leq 1,\\ -1\\leq v\\leq 4\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle.](images/66/666f6d0c04138db1649106b89e6383d1e2860dcde88e6dd25c5413612af5e704.png)\n\n**Step 3: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}3&-1 \\\\ 1&-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Take the reciprocal and its magnitude.** The inverse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{u}{2} - \\frac{v}{2}, \\\\ y&=\\frac{u}{2} - \\frac{3 v}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant is nonzero, so this linear inverse is defined everywhere. Area uses the absolute value of the reciprocal determinant:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\left|\\frac{1}{-2}\\right|=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-1}^{4}\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left(1-(-1)\\right)\\cdot\\left(4-(-1)\\right) \\\\ &=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724838 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 y&=5 x - 3,\\qquad 3 y=5 x - 8, \\\\ 4 x&=2 y - 1,\\qquad 4 x=2 y + 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1: Choose coordinates.** Rewrite each boundary using its shared expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 x - 3 y&=3,\\qquad 5 x - 3 y=8, \\\\ 4 x - 2 y&=-1,\\qquad 4 x - 2 y=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $u=5 x - 3 y$ and $v=4 x - 2 y$.\n\n**Step 2: Map the boundaries.** The four boundaries become $u=3$, $u=8$, $v=-1$, and $v=4$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\leq u\\leq 8,\\ -1\\leq v\\leq 4\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle.](images/7d/7d3a235807027c0a82f61ae54f940338b435b929976f8de871e226e25f52a600.png)\n\n**Step 3: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}5&-3 \\\\ 4&-2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Take the reciprocal and its magnitude.** The inverse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=- u + \\frac{3 v}{2}, \\\\ y&=- 2 u + \\frac{5 v}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant is nonzero, so this linear inverse is defined everywhere. Area uses the absolute value of the reciprocal determinant:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\left|\\frac{1}{2}\\right|=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-1}^{4}\\int_{3}^{8}1\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left(8-(3)\\right)\\cdot\\left(4-(-1)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724839 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 y&=3 x - 3,\\qquad 2 y=3 x - 8, \\\\ 2 x&=3 y - 3,\\qquad 2 x=3 y - 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1: Choose coordinates.** Rewrite each boundary using its shared expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 x - 2 y&=3,\\qquad 3 x - 2 y=8, \\\\ 2 x - 3 y&=-3,\\qquad 2 x - 3 y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $u=3 x - 2 y$ and $v=2 x - 3 y$.\n\n**Step 2: Map the boundaries.** The four boundaries become $u=3$, $u=8$, $v=-3$, and $v=-2$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\leq u\\leq 8,\\ -3\\leq v\\leq -2\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle.](images/09/0959b84849d391c6f3eb3e9ae73157d5ee65a51030233441533acdde2eace96e.png)\n\n**Step 3: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}3&-2 \\\\ 2&-3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Take the reciprocal and its magnitude.** The inverse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{3 u}{5} - \\frac{2 v}{5}, \\\\ y&=\\frac{2 u}{5} - \\frac{3 v}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant is nonzero, so this linear inverse is defined everywhere. Area uses the absolute value of the reciprocal determinant:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\left|\\frac{1}{-5}\\right|=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{5}\\int_{-3}^{-2}\\int_{3}^{8}1\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot\\left(8-(3)\\right)\\cdot\\left(-2-(-3)\\right) \\\\ &=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724841 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=2 x - 1,\\qquad y=2 x - 5, \\\\ x&=2 y + 3,\\qquad x=2 y + 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1: Choose coordinates.** Rewrite each boundary using its shared expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 x - y&=1,\\qquad 2 x - y=5, \\\\ x - 2 y&=3,\\qquad x - 2 y=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $u=2 x - y$ and $v=x - 2 y$.\n\n**Step 2: Map the boundaries.** The four boundaries become $u=1$, $u=5$, $v=3$, and $v=7$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\leq u\\leq 5,\\ 3\\leq v\\leq 7\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle.](images/15/154c9ec2df4b1498040f6230c4eb999be5c4bddf3fda5225a63383cbd94f52c2.png)\n\n**Step 3: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}2&-1 \\\\ 1&-2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Take the reciprocal and its magnitude.** The inverse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{2 u}{3} - \\frac{v}{3}, \\\\ y&=\\frac{u}{3} - \\frac{2 v}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant is nonzero, so this linear inverse is defined everywhere. Area uses the absolute value of the reciprocal determinant:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\left|\\frac{1}{-3}\\right|=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{3}^{7}\\int_{1}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot\\left(5-(1)\\right)\\cdot\\left(7-(3)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724856 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=3 x - 3,\\qquad y=3 x - 4, \\\\ x&=3 y - 2,\\qquad x=3 y - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1: Choose coordinates.** Rewrite each boundary using its shared expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 x - y&=3,\\qquad 3 x - y=4, \\\\ x - 3 y&=-2,\\qquad x - 3 y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $u=3 x - y$ and $v=x - 3 y$.\n\n**Step 2: Map the boundaries.** The four boundaries become $u=3$, $u=4$, $v=-2$, and $v=-1$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\leq u\\leq 4,\\ -2\\leq v\\leq -1\\}.\n$$\n\n![The transformed rectangle.](images/31/31a33026b52241892a59844eb75067f8fa4c5fc47e17054cf31d358ccdb9cb7a.png)\n\n**Step 3: Compute the forward Jacobian.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}3&-1 \\\\ 1&-3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Take the reciprocal and its magnitude.** The inverse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{3 u}{8} - \\frac{v}{8}, \\\\ y&=\\frac{u}{8} - \\frac{3 v}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant is nonzero, so this linear inverse is defined everywhere. Area uses the absolute value of the reciprocal determinant:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\left|\\frac{1}{-8}\\right|=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5: Integrate over the rectangle.**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\iint_{\\Delta}\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{8}\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\int_{3}^{4}1\\,\\mathrm du\\,\\mathrm dv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot\\left(4-(3)\\right)\\cdot\\left(-1-(-2)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724869 :question/problem "![](images/29/2992cef2d7e9f4bbb9c208dca4abcd6749bf196b4fc7b609c6afa119a705a72f.png)\n\nThe curve shown above is defined by\n\n$$\nx=t^4+t+1,\\qquad y=t^2+1,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point where $t=-1$. The dashed line is the normal at $C$. Determine the coordinates of the point where this normal crosses the $x$-axis. Use the equations to obtain exact values.\n\nThe normal crosses the $x$-axis at $(${{field-1}}, {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=-1$ to find $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=(-1)^4+(-1)+1=1,\\\\\ny&=(-1)^2+1=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $C=(1,2)$. Differentiate the two coordinates:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4t^3+1,\\qquad y'(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t}{4t^3+1}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate it at $t=-1$:\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2(-1)}{4(-1)^3+1}=\\frac{-2}{-3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the normal is the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{2/3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(1,2)$:\n\n$$\ny-2=-\\frac{3}{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute this and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2&=-\\frac{3}{2}(x-1),\\\\\nx-1&=\\frac{4}{3},\\\\\nx&=\\frac{7}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the normal crosses the $x$-axis at $\\left(\\dfrac{7}{3},0\\right)$."}]}
